r|lll}\n  & Df & F value & Pr(>F)\\\\\n  & <int> & <dbl> & <dbl>\\\\\n\\hline\n\tgroup &    2 & 2.328885 & 0.09747347\\\\\n\t  & 7640 &       NA &         NA\\\\\n\\end{tabular}\n","text/plain":"      Df   F value  Pr(>F)    \ngroup    2 2.328885 0.09747347\n      7640       NA         NA"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"#your answer here : \nBefore we can perform ANOVA, we must check one more critical **assumption homogeneity of variances\nusing the Levene's test.**\nTherefore in the test above we can see that the **p-value is greater than 0.05**\nThis means we do **not** reject the null hypothesis. This means the variances are considered equal.\nWe can proceed with the ANOVA test.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"anova_result <- aov(Survival_Outcome ~ Metabolism_Rate, data = cancer_data)\nsummary(anova_result)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-03T18:01:33.210342Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-03T18:01:33.212101Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-03T18:01:33.234523Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-03T18:01:33.232636Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"                  Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value   Pr(>F)    \nMetabolism_Rate    2    106   53.04   23.18 9.21e-11 ***\nResiduals       7640  17482    2.29                     \n---\nSignif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":10},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"As observed in the above test since the P-value (under Pr(>F)) **is far below 0.05** we can confidently **reject the null hypothesis**. Which in turn means that all group means are equal. This implies that metabolism rate does have a significant effect on survival outcome.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Why are we not using the t-test?**\nA t-test is used to compare the means of only **two groups**. In our dataset, the Metabolism_Rate variable has three distinct groups.\n**ANOVA** is the correct method because it is specifically designed to compare the means of three or more groups simultaneously in a single test, thereby controlling the overall Type I error rate.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Post Hoc Tests\n\n* When you use ANOVA to test the equality of at least three group means, statistically significant results indicate that not all of the group means are equal. However, ANOVA results do not identify which particular differences between pairs of means are significant.\n\n* To identify which particular group's means differ from each other , we use Post Hoc Tests.\n\n* The most common method is Tukey's method.\n\n* If the adjusted p-value between a pair of groups is less than 0.05, then we can assume statistical significance between the pair. (difference !)\n\n* You can learn more about this [here](https://statisticsbyjim.com/anova/post-hoc-tests-anova/)\n\n* NOTE: If p-value is 0.000 then it means that the value too small to be printed out and must be considered as <0.05","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"*Problem 3*\n\nPerform Tukey's Test to infer which pairs have means statistically significant from one other and which don't. Which pair of categories show a clear difference in the patient's survival ? (1 point)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"tukey_results <- TukeyHSD(anova_result)\nprint(tukey_results)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-03T18:04:47.825028Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-03T18:04:47.826829Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-03T18:04:47.861037Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-03T18:04:47.857796Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"  Tukey multiple comparisons of means\n    95% family-wise confidence level\n\nFit: aov(formula = Survival_Outcome ~ Metabolism_Rate, data = cancer_data)\n\n$Metabolism_Rate\n          diff         lwr       upr     p adj\n1-0 0.20494356  0.10674008 0.3031470 0.0000030\n2-0 0.26640841  0.16862594 0.3641909 0.0000000\n2-1 0.06146485 -0.04288722 0.1658169 0.3510579\n\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":12},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"1-0 and 2-0 categories show a clear difference in a patient's survival. This can be explained by thier p-values. As we can observe, the values for 1-0 and 2-0 are below 0.05 hence show a clear difference.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Two Way ANOVA\n\n* Two-way ANOVA is used when the response variable is influenced by several factors.\n* Now along with metabolism we want to consider the factor whether the patient has taken this new drug or not.\n* Two-way ANOVA tests for 3 conclusions :\n    * If the metabolism rate affects the patient's survival ?\n    * If the drug has any effect on the patient's survival ?\n    * If the interaction between the two independent variables (metabolism rate and drug intake) affects the patient's survival ?\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"*Problem 4*\n\nDoes the interaction between the different metabolism rates and drug intake significantly affect the patient's survival ? Justify your answer.\nBased on this study , would recommend this drug based on the patient's metabolism ? \nWhat would you name this new drug 👀 ? (2 points)","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"cancer_data$Drug_take <- as.factor(cancer_data$Drug_take)\ntwo_way_anova_result <- aov(Survival_Outcome ~ Metabolism_Rate * Drug_take, data = cancer_data)\nsummary(two_way_anova_result)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-03T18:25:35.593601Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-03T18:25:35.595897Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-03T18:25:35.633663Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-03T18:25:35.630745Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"                            Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value   Pr(>F)    \nMetabolism_Rate              2    106   53.04  23.204 8.98e-11 ***\nDrug_take                    1     26   25.54  11.174 0.000833 ***\nMetabolism_Rate:Drug_take    2      1    0.46   0.203 0.816446    \nResiduals                 7637  17455    2.29                     \n---\nSignif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":15},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Since the p-value is greater than 0.05 for the row Metabolism_Rate:Drug_take, we can conclude that there is **no significant interaction** and drug is consistent across all metabolism groups.\n**No** I would **not** recommend this drug based on the patient's metabolism. \nWe can name this drug 'Cance-stop'.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Principal Component Analysis","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"*Problem 5*\n\n* Explain Dimensionality Reduction and how does it impact the curse of dimensionality ?(think in terms of data sparsity) Perform PCA on the Columns of the gene expression(Gene_1 to Gene_15). Extract how much variance does each principal component explain. How many components should we retain based on the scree plot.(1+1+1 points)\n\n* Examine the loadings of the first two principal components. Which variables contribute most to these components? (1 point)\n\n* Hint 1: Scree Plot is a common method for determining the number of PCs to be retained through a graphical representation. A Scree Plot is a simple line segment plot that shows the eigenvalues for each individual PC.\n\n* Hint 2: In PCA, the contribution of each feature to a principal component is called the loading. Loadings are compared with the absolute value\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Dimensionality Reduction** is the process of reducing the number of features or dimensions in a dataset while retaining as much of the important information as possible. Instead of working with 15 different gene columns, we might try to summarize them into 2 or 3 new, artificial variables called principal components. The **curse of dimensionality** is the problem that arises when you work with data in many dimensions. Due to the increase in the dimensions the data points become very far apart as called **Data Sparsity**.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"gene_data <- cancer_data %>%\n  select(starts_with(\"Gene_\"))\nscaled_gene_data <- scale(gene_data)\npca_result <- prcomp(scaled_gene_data)\nsummary(pca_result)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-03T18:25:42.969693Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-03T18:25:42.985554Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-03T18:25:43.072412Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-03T18:25:43.066467Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"Importance of components:\n                          PC1    PC2    PC3     PC4     PC5     PC6     PC7\nStandard deviation     2.3752 2.0000 1.7794 0.91512 0.56616 0.41582 0.39179\nProportion of Variance 0.3761 0.2667 0.2111 0.05583 0.02137 0.01153 0.01023\nCumulative Proportion  0.3761 0.6428 0.8538 0.90966 0.93103 0.94256 0.95279\n                           PC8     PC9   PC10    PC11    PC12    PC13    PC14\nStandard deviation     0.38064 0.34874 0.3264 0.29776 0.27982 0.26026 0.22822\nProportion of Variance 0.00966 0.00811 0.0071 0.00591 0.00522 0.00452 0.00347\nCumulative Proportion  0.96245 0.97056 0.9777 0.98357 0.98879 0.99331 0.99678\n                          PC15\nStandard deviation     0.21972\nProportion of Variance 0.00322\nCumulative Proportion  1.00000"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":16},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The 'summary(pca_result)' explains the variance of each principal component.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"variance_explained <- data.frame(\n  PC = 1:15,\n  Variance = pca_result$sdev^2 / sum(pca_result$sdev^2)\n)\n\nggplot(variance_explained, aes(x = PC, y = Variance)) +\n  geom_col(fill = \"black\") +\n  geom_line(group = 1, color = \"red\") +\n  geom_point(color = \"red\", size = 2) +\n  labs(ti